  <AboutText>k__BackingField: '<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>

    Spiderette is
    a popular game combining the layout of Klondike and the mechanics of Spider.
    It is designed for quick games and requires a standard 52-card deck, with 28
    arranged into a 7 column staircase.



    Spiderette requires players to sort
    cards into same-suit stacks from King to Ace. Cards are arranged on the board
    in descending order, so players must manoeuvre the cards until they have a complete
    same-suit stack, causing those cards to be removed from the game. Victory is
    achieved when all 52 cards have been sorted. Spiderette is primarily a skill-based
    game, but luck still plays a part in whether or not you''ll win.



    <b><size=+10>History</size></b>

    Spiderette
    is very young, having been introduced alongside the modern version of Spider
    in the 1949 book "The Complete Book of Solitaire and Patience Games" written
    by Albert H. Morehead and Geoffrey Mott-Smith. The name "Spiderette" literally
    means "Little Spider" which is exactly what the game is.



    <b><size=+10>Today</size></b>

    Spiderette
    is loved in the same way that Spider is, though some prefer Spiderette for its
    faster games. It has several variants such as one-suit to adjust for different
    skill levels. Spiderette''s popularity has grown alongside Spider''s, offering
    players the same experience in a much shorter time.'
  <GuideText>k__BackingField: 
